With a little bit of fiddling, I've been able to get this down to this:

```
       let is_context_match = match_regex(&kube_ctx, &context_config.context_pattern);
        if !is_context_match {
            return false;
        };
        if let Some(user_pattern) = context_config.user_pattern {
            if let Some(user) = kube_user.as_ref() {
                return match_regex(user, user_pattern);
            } else {
                // If there is a user pattern but no user, the pattern cannot match
                return false;
            }
        } else {
            // If there is no user pattern, then this context config matches
            return true;
        }
```

with the following definition of `match_regex`:
```
fn match_regex(target: &str, pattern: &str) -> bool {
    match regex::Regex::new(pattern) {
        Ok(re) => re.is_match(&target),
        Err(error) => {
            log::warn!("Could not compile regex pattern `{}`:\n{}", pattern, error);
            false
        }
    }
}
```

I'm still not entirely happy with this, because:

1. The `match_regex` name is terrible.
2. I think the body of the closure in `find` can still be cleaned up a little bit
3. I think we can modify match_regex to do capture group parsing and output in a single pass instead of having to do two passes

but I think it's a starting point.

