I think it's more likely due to my inexperience with rust :-/

> Why do we take two Option<&str> and then an Option<&String>?

Mostly because the inputs were this and this version compiled :-/ I still haven't completely wrapped my head around when to use &str vs String vs &String...

> What do these strings represent? (Every time I think I have it, one of them falls out of my head and I have to start thinking about it again).

I think of this function as a wrapper around a regex search and replace: have a pattern, have a current value, have a replacement. The alias is the replacement. Only difference: if the pattern doesn't match, return None instead of current value.

Maybe the problem arises from the fact that this is both s&r and matching: I need the None to indicate no match, but the replacement to not compile the pattern twice.

> Why does the one of the call sites have a Some(Some(something, _, _)) followed immediately by an unwrap_or_else?

Again, probably because I got it compiled like that when using the find_map (if you mean line 201) :-(

Just to clarify, the part around line 201:

```
   let (matched_context_config, display_context, display_user) = config.contexts.iter().find_map(|context_config| {
        let Some(context_alias) = get_aliased_name(Some(context_config.context_pattern), Some(&current_kube_ctx_name), context_config.context_alias) else {
            return None;
        };

        let user_alias = ... 
...
        Some((Some(context_config), context_alias, user_alias))
    }).unwrap_or_else(|| (None, current_kube_ctx_name.clone(), ctx_components.user));

```

So `Some(Some(something, _, _))` is actually `Some(Some(something), _, _)`,, so `Option<Tuple<Option<..>,..>>` which hopefully is a bit more sane than an `Option<Option<Tuple>>`. I need the Option because the find_map wants it.

>  but I suspect there's a way to make this simpler than it currently is.

I sure hope, but I'm currently out of my wits/experience for anything to make this easier. :-(

